00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:43.790
APARNA SINHA: I'm Aparna Sinha,
product manager for Kubernetes

00:49:43.790 --> 00:49:45.620
and Google Container Engine.

00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:48.250
There has been a dramatic
shift in the last couple

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:51.150
of years in the
ability for enterprises

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:53.780
to realize the
speed, efficiency,

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:56.970
and scale benefits of
distributed computing,

00:49:56.970 --> 00:50:00.910
whether it is in the form of
a private cloud on premise

00:50:00.910 --> 00:50:03.690
or public clouds at
the scale of Google.

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:06.170
container-packaged
apps are legitimately

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:09.650
portable and fast to deploy
because the packaging

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:13.760
format provides independence
from the underlying host file

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:16.170
system and OS distribution.

00:50:16.170 --> 00:50:19.950
So you can deploy an app
quickly and take it anywhere.

00:50:19.950 --> 00:50:22.970
But containers are only
part of the solution.

00:50:22.970 --> 00:50:26.460
Running applications in a
sea of distributed resources

00:50:26.460 --> 00:50:28.230
poses new challenges.

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:31.240
For example, how do
you schedule containers

00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:34.770
to utilize the underlying
resources efficiently when

00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:37.340
those resources are unreliable?

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:39.940
How do you automatically
monitor and recover

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:43.320
containers and applications
when they fail?

00:50:43.320 --> 00:50:45.690
How do you add and
remove containers

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.580
to scale the
application with demand?

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:50.700
And with everything
moving around,

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:54.770
how do clients discover
containerized applications?

00:50:54.770 --> 00:50:58.620
Google open sourced Kubernetes
to provide a solution

00:50:58.620 --> 00:51:02.050
for these and other distributed
computing challenges

00:51:02.050 --> 00:51:05.950
based on over 12 years
of experience internally.

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:11.590
Out of the box, Kubernetes
automates container scheduling,

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:16.530
health checking and recovery,
replication to ensure up time,

00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:20.260
and internal network management
to ensure service naming,

00:51:20.260 --> 00:51:22.860
discovery, and load balancing.

00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:25.320
The basic concepts
Kubernetes uses

00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:29.890
to manage container clusters
include a pod, which

00:51:29.890 --> 00:51:33.620
is a group of containers that
are always scheduled together

00:51:33.620 --> 00:51:36.600
and have a shared networking
and storage identity.

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:40.290
Pods typically bundle an app
with its helper functions,

00:51:40.290 --> 00:51:43.210
for example, a web
server and a file puller.

00:51:43.210 --> 00:51:45.510
A controller,
which is a feedback

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:49.900
loop that drives current
state towards desired state.

00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:54.040
This is the basis for automation
at many levels in Kubernetes.

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:57.060
For example,
replication controllers

00:51:57.060 --> 00:51:59.710
ensure service
continuity by making

00:51:59.710 --> 00:52:04.010
sure a sufficient number of
pods are running at all times.

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:07.490
Controllers are also
used for auto-scaling.

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:11.450
A service is an abstraction
across pods that together

00:52:11.450 --> 00:52:13.560
represent an application.

00:52:13.560 --> 00:52:17.620
A service has a stable IP
address for the external world,

00:52:17.620 --> 00:52:21.320
thereby hiding the complexity
of dynamically scheduled pods

00:52:21.320 --> 00:52:22.980
across a cluster.

00:52:22.980 --> 00:52:26.970
Services enable application
discovery, networking,

00:52:26.970 --> 00:52:28.390
and load balancing.

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:32.880
Lastly, labels are
user-defined metadata

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:36.480
that can be attached to
any object in Kubernetes.

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:40.200
Selectors can then be used
to query the object's state

00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:42.650
and define policies.

00:52:42.650 --> 00:52:47.210
This enables many different
types of management operations,

00:52:47.210 --> 00:52:51.580
for example, automated Canary
deployments or rolling updates

00:52:51.580 --> 00:52:53.520
to name just two.

00:52:53.520 --> 00:52:57.900
Our goal with Kubernetes is to
meet customers where they are.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:00.720
Containers help you
deploy to any cloud.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:03.730
Kubernetes gives you a
management stack that

00:53:03.730 --> 00:53:06.770
can go anywhere containers can.

00:53:06.770 --> 00:53:10.090
The freedom to deploy
on premise, in hybrid,

00:53:10.090 --> 00:53:12.840
or multi-cloud
environments lets customers

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:16.810
choose based on the merits
of the underlying cloud.

00:53:16.810 --> 00:53:20.680
Google Container Engine
provides the performance, scale,

00:53:20.680 --> 00:53:23.350
and efficiency of the
Google Cloud platform

00:53:23.350 --> 00:53:25.580
to Kubernetes customers.

00:53:25.580 --> 00:53:28.250
It is one of the
easiest, fastest,

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:31.790
and most cost effective ways to
manage containers in production

00:53:31.790 --> 00:53:33.630
today.

00:53:33.630 --> 00:53:35.570
This is why thousands
of customers

00:53:35.570 --> 00:53:37.330
run their production
environments

00:53:37.330 --> 00:53:39.560
with Google Container Engine.

00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:43.750
Now I would like to invite one
of those customers, Tyler Davis

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:47.820
from porch.com to talk about
how Google Container Engine has

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:50.120
impacted their business.

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:53.401
TYLER DAVIS: I'm Tyler Davis,
DevOps engineer with porch.com.

00:53:53.401 --> 00:53:55.570
Porch has collected a
massive amount of data

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:57.630
to provide transparency
to the home improvement

00:53:57.630 --> 00:54:00.120
industry on who is the
best professional to hire.

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:03.282
With terabytes of data from
both private and public sources,

00:54:03.282 --> 00:54:04.740
Porch is able to
connect homeowners

00:54:04.740 --> 00:54:06.290
to the highest
quality professionals

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:08.180
through the Porch platform.

00:54:08.180 --> 00:54:11.910
The Porch platform is present
in over 1,700 Lowes stores

00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:14.890
and available via the Porch
retail solution on websites

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:17.230
like wayfair.com
and atgstores.com.

00:54:17.230 --> 00:54:20.430
For Porch, our
engineering objectives

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:22.360
start with stability
and reliability.

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:25.130
Website up time and
reliability are paramount.

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:28.560
No website means no customers,
which means no revenue.

00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:31.380
Similarly, developer
time is at a premium.

00:54:31.380 --> 00:54:34.560
Kubernetes ease of deployment
accelerates everything.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:36.440
Kubernetes reduces
the time and overhead

00:54:36.440 --> 00:54:38.790
for developers during new
service bootstrapping,

00:54:38.790 --> 00:54:40.840
rolling out bug fixes
and new features,

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:42.340
and reduces maintenance time.

00:54:42.340 --> 00:54:46.090
With Kubernetes, we fail
fast, but we fix it faster.

00:54:46.090 --> 00:54:49.260
Before December 2014, Porch
had a monolithic service

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:50.330
architecture.

00:54:50.330 --> 00:54:52.670
As this crude diagram
shows, we provisioned

00:54:52.670 --> 00:54:55.980
bare virtual machines running
specific applications, often

00:54:55.980 --> 00:54:58.770
with less than 10% utilization
due to over provisioning

00:54:58.770 --> 00:55:00.610
for unexpected demand.

00:55:00.610 --> 00:55:03.230
Also, there were load balancers
for each application creating

00:55:03.230 --> 00:55:05.190
multiple layers
of load balancers,

00:55:05.190 --> 00:55:08.310
confusing troubleshooting
of network or code problems.

00:55:08.310 --> 00:55:11.070
Deployments could take one
to two hours on average,

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:13.770
sometimes half a day or
longer, with inconsistent state

00:55:13.770 --> 00:55:15.570
for the duration of the rollout.

00:55:15.570 --> 00:55:18.040
This was an inefficient
and ultimately expensive

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:19.580
implementation.

00:55:19.580 --> 00:55:21.664
Under our new architecture,
client connections

00:55:21.664 --> 00:55:23.080
route through a
single Google load

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:25.850
balancer directly into
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:55:25.850 --> 00:55:28.490
There's only one load
balancer instead of up to four

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:31.210
due to Kubernetes ability to
proxy traffic between services

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:32.390
internally.

00:55:32.390 --> 00:55:35.110
This actually helped us discover
bugs in our own applications

00:55:35.110 --> 00:55:38.240
which were masked by the layers
of load balancers before.

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:40.115
We currently run over
100 different services

00:55:40.115 --> 00:55:41.590
in the cluster.

00:55:41.590 --> 00:55:43.440
These applications
are mostly stateless,

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:45.340
synchronizing between
other services,

00:55:45.340 --> 00:55:47.550
and storing state on
databases or Memcache

00:55:47.550 --> 00:55:49.380
outside the cluster.

00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:51.460
The large multi-terabyte
databases,

00:55:51.460 --> 00:55:53.630
configuration management
systems like Chef,

00:55:53.630 --> 00:55:55.210
and other strictly
stateful systems

00:55:55.210 --> 00:55:58.460
exist outside the cluster
on compute engine VMs.

00:55:58.460 --> 00:56:00.160
Redis and other
stateful services

00:56:00.160 --> 00:56:01.750
are being migrated
into the cluster

00:56:01.750 --> 00:56:04.650
as we get more comfortable
with GKE features.

00:56:04.650 --> 00:56:07.000
As a result, Porch enjoys
the following architecture

00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:09.210
benefits-- developer
independence

00:56:09.210 --> 00:56:11.420
as self-service architecture
allows developers

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:13.680
to go for commit to
deployed in production

00:56:13.680 --> 00:56:16.890
in less than 10 minutes
without DevOps' involvement.

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:19.600
Deployments are reliable and
can be rolled out or rolled back

00:56:19.600 --> 00:56:21.670
in three minutes or less.

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:23.900
Due to the independent
promotion architecture,

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:27.300
we can and do deploy as
many as 30 to 50 times a day

00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:30.910
or more with almost no
risk and no downtime.

00:56:30.910 --> 00:56:33.670
Finally, Kubernetes allows
us to sleep through events

00:56:33.670 --> 00:56:35.970
that would otherwise
require a 2:00 AM conference

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:37.171
call in engineering.

00:56:37.171 --> 00:56:39.670
Having worked with Kubernetes
for over a year in production,

00:56:39.670 --> 00:56:41.324
we've learned a few things.

00:56:41.324 --> 00:56:42.740
Create deployment
containers which

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:44.820
only include the minimum
necessary artifacts

00:56:44.820 --> 00:56:45.790
and run time.

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:47.950
This will lead to faster
deployments and easier

00:56:47.950 --> 00:56:50.740
reasoning about deployment
or application failures.

00:56:50.740 --> 00:56:52.820
Also, don't expect
Kubernetes to be anything

00:56:52.820 --> 00:56:54.530
like what has come before.

00:56:54.530 --> 00:56:56.270
Treat your applications
like cattle.

00:56:56.270 --> 00:56:58.616
Design for failures and
redundancy in the app.

00:56:58.616 --> 00:57:00.740
And leverage all the
Kubernetes features available,

00:57:00.740 --> 00:57:04.180
such as service discovery,
deployments, and health checks.

00:57:04.180 --> 00:57:06.880
When in doubt, experiment.

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:08.030
Thank you for joining us.

00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:10.590
I hope these tips can help you
to quickly and reliably scale

00:57:10.590 --> 00:57:11.512
your own architecture.

00:57:11.512 --> 00:57:13.720
To learn more,
visit kubernetes.io.

00:57:13.720 --> 00:57:17.470
